        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/11/1058">

	<title>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 1058: Mask-Guided Foreground&amp;ndash;Background Contrastive Learning for Text-Guided COVID-19 Segmentation in Chest X-Ray and CT Images</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/11/1058</link>
	<description>Text-guided segmentation combines visual information with semantic descriptions, but semantic conditioning alone does not explicitly supervise the separation of foreground and background representations. We combine a VGG16-based encoder&amp;amp;ndash;decoder and the established cross-position attention pathway with a two-layer CLIP text adapter and mask-guided foreground&amp;amp;ndash;background contrastive supervision at three encoder levels. Reference masks guide contrastive sampling during training but are not inference inputs. On the fixed QaTa-COV19 chest X-ray benchmark, three independent runs yielded a Dice of 0.8589&amp;amp;plusmn;0.0037 and a mean foreground intersection over union (mIoU) of 0.7583&amp;amp;plusmn;0.0045; on the MosMedData+ two-dimensional CT-slice benchmark, the corresponding results were 0.8023&amp;amp;plusmn;0.0038 and 0.6745&amp;amp;plusmn;0.0044 (mean&amp;amp;plusmn;standard deviation). A two-factor ablation assessed the text adapter and contrastive term separately and jointly. Individual-negative InfoNCE yielded lower mean overlap scores than the proposed average-negative objective under the matched training settings. Inference-time left/right exchanges in unilateral-description subsets revealed substantial sensitivity to incorrect laterality. These findings describe fixed image/slice-level benchmarks and do not establish external clinical generalization.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-22</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 1058: Mask-Guided Foreground&amp;ndash;Background Contrastive Learning for Text-Guided COVID-19 Segmentation in Chest X-Ray and CT Images</b></p>
	<p>Information <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/11/1058">doi: 10.3390/info17111058</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Baoyang Han
		Jia Wei
		</p>
	<p>Text-guided segmentation combines visual information with semantic descriptions, but semantic conditioning alone does not explicitly supervise the separation of foreground and background representations. We combine a VGG16-based encoder&amp;amp;ndash;decoder and the established cross-position attention pathway with a two-layer CLIP text adapter and mask-guided foreground&amp;amp;ndash;background contrastive supervision at three encoder levels. Reference masks guide contrastive sampling during training but are not inference inputs. On the fixed QaTa-COV19 chest X-ray benchmark, three independent runs yielded a Dice of 0.8589&amp;amp;plusmn;0.0037 and a mean foreground intersection over union (mIoU) of 0.7583&amp;amp;plusmn;0.0045; on the MosMedData+ two-dimensional CT-slice benchmark, the corresponding results were 0.8023&amp;amp;plusmn;0.0038 and 0.6745&amp;amp;plusmn;0.0044 (mean&amp;amp;plusmn;standard deviation). A two-factor ablation assessed the text adapter and contrastive term separately and jointly. Individual-negative InfoNCE yielded lower mean overlap scores than the proposed average-negative objective under the matched training settings. Inference-time left/right exchanges in unilateral-description subsets revealed substantial sensitivity to incorrect laterality. These findings describe fixed image/slice-level benchmarks and do not establish external clinical generalization.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>Mask-Guided Foreground&amp;amp;ndash;Background Contrastive Learning for Text-Guided COVID-19 Segmentation in Chest X-Ray and CT Images</dc:title>
