        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/11/1054">

	<title>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 1054: One-Sided Residual Certificates for Exact Quantized Image Retrieval</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/11/1054</link>
	<description>Exact-score retrieval returns the same ordered top-k and numerical scores as a specified exhaustive computation. Quantized vectors can screen candidates, but their approximation errors require safe upper bounds. We study a one-sided residual certificate, denoted OARC+, which stores the positive-part residual norm for nonnegative queries, replacing the full residual norm at identical payload. The inequality is a direct consequence of Cauchy&amp;amp;ndash;Schwarz; the contribution is its single-scalar specialization, exact-score integration, and controlled resource comparison. Exploratory experiments on 900 Digits gallery items and 357 queries reduced mean refinements by 22.05% for pixels and 16.49% for histograms of oriented gradients (HOG). A revision-stage 4K Large Scale Dataset and Benchmark (4KLSDB) transfer evaluation used 129,484 gallery images and 2000 validation queries, with reported reductions of 46.46% for HOG and 48.71% for HOG combined with hue, saturation, and value histograms (HOG+HSV). The test split remains unused. We characterize when tighter bounds cross the stopping threshold and model the cost needed for refinement savings to offset scanning and sorting. Signed queries require a different certificate, and cached exhaustive scoring was faster on Digits. The evidence concerns certificate selectivity under fixed representations; large-gallery latency, physical input/output (I/O) savings, and confirmatory generalization remain unestablished.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-22</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 1054: One-Sided Residual Certificates for Exact Quantized Image Retrieval</b></p>
	<p>Information <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/11/1054">doi: 10.3390/info17111054</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Bowen Feng
		</p>
	<p>Exact-score retrieval returns the same ordered top-k and numerical scores as a specified exhaustive computation. Quantized vectors can screen candidates, but their approximation errors require safe upper bounds. We study a one-sided residual certificate, denoted OARC+, which stores the positive-part residual norm for nonnegative queries, replacing the full residual norm at identical payload. The inequality is a direct consequence of Cauchy&amp;amp;ndash;Schwarz; the contribution is its single-scalar specialization, exact-score integration, and controlled resource comparison. Exploratory experiments on 900 Digits gallery items and 357 queries reduced mean refinements by 22.05% for pixels and 16.49% for histograms of oriented gradients (HOG). A revision-stage 4K Large Scale Dataset and Benchmark (4KLSDB) transfer evaluation used 129,484 gallery images and 2000 validation queries, with reported reductions of 46.46% for HOG and 48.71% for HOG combined with hue, saturation, and value histograms (HOG+HSV). The test split remains unused. We characterize when tighter bounds cross the stopping threshold and model the cost needed for refinement savings to offset scanning and sorting. Signed queries require a different certificate, and cached exhaustive scoring was faster on Digits. The evidence concerns certificate selectivity under fixed representations; large-gallery latency, physical input/output (I/O) savings, and confirmatory generalization remain unestablished.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>One-Sided Residual Certificates for Exact Quantized Image Retrieval</dc:title>
