        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1050">

	<title>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 1050: Thermal-Aware Generative Adversarial Augmentation for Small-Sample Induction-Motor Fault Detection Using Infrared Thermography</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1050</link>
	<description>Fault diagnosis in induction motors is challenging when only a limited number of labeled infrared thermal images are available for training. Effective small-sample augmentation requires generated images to preserve class-related appearance patterns as well as visual realism. To address this challenge, a thermal-aware generative adversarial network (TA-GAN) is proposed for induction-motor fault diagnosis using pseudo-colour infrared images. An adaptive attention module combines feature intensity and Sobel-gradient information to emphasize component&amp;amp;ndash;state-related thermal patterns. A dual-branch critic jointly evaluates global and attention-weighted features, while an appearance-consistency loss constrains bright-region intensity, gradient structure, and spatial position during generation. The trained generator augments the limited real samples, and the combined dataset is used to train a lightweight classifier. Experiments on acquisition-batch-separated data partitions show improved classification performance over the evaluated real-only baselines under matched classifier-training budgets. The complete framework performs best among the evaluated component and individual-loss configurations. Generated-sample evaluation further indicates improved appearance similarity to real training images compared with a basic conditional GAN, while evaluation on a different motor provides additional evidence of transfer under the tested conditions. Image generation is performed offline, allowing the deployed system to retain the lightweight MobileNetV4 classifier architecture.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-21</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 1050: Thermal-Aware Generative Adversarial Augmentation for Small-Sample Induction-Motor Fault Detection Using Infrared Thermography</b></p>
	<p>Information <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1050">doi: 10.3390/info17101050</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Xu Gui
		Fan Yang
		Jian Zhang
		Wei Ding
		Yibo Tang
		Shuai Mao
		Pan Hu
		</p>
	<p>Fault diagnosis in induction motors is challenging when only a limited number of labeled infrared thermal images are available for training. Effective small-sample augmentation requires generated images to preserve class-related appearance patterns as well as visual realism. To address this challenge, a thermal-aware generative adversarial network (TA-GAN) is proposed for induction-motor fault diagnosis using pseudo-colour infrared images. An adaptive attention module combines feature intensity and Sobel-gradient information to emphasize component&amp;amp;ndash;state-related thermal patterns. A dual-branch critic jointly evaluates global and attention-weighted features, while an appearance-consistency loss constrains bright-region intensity, gradient structure, and spatial position during generation. The trained generator augments the limited real samples, and the combined dataset is used to train a lightweight classifier. Experiments on acquisition-batch-separated data partitions show improved classification performance over the evaluated real-only baselines under matched classifier-training budgets. The complete framework performs best among the evaluated component and individual-loss configurations. Generated-sample evaluation further indicates improved appearance similarity to real training images compared with a basic conditional GAN, while evaluation on a different motor provides additional evidence of transfer under the tested conditions. Image generation is performed offline, allowing the deployed system to retain the lightweight MobileNetV4 classifier architecture.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>Thermal-Aware Generative Adversarial Augmentation for Small-Sample Induction-Motor Fault Detection Using Infrared Thermography</dc:title>
