        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1042">

	<title>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 1042: A Dated Corpus and a Seven-Layer Benchmark for Kazakhstani Legal Question Answering</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1042</link>
	<description>Legal question answering is usually evaluated as if the law were static. In Kazakhstan, it changed twice within one year: a new Constitution replaced the 1995 text on 1 July 2026, and a new Tax Code replaced its predecessor on 1 January 2026, both reusing article numbers for different norms. We release a dated corpus of 41 Kazakhstani acts parsed to article level in both official languages (12,235 Russian and 11,973 Kazakh articles) and a benchmark of 150 question-answer pairs in seven layers, two of which score declining to answer and asking for a date. Two practising lawyers confirmed every pair. Comparing a no-retrieval baseline, retrieval-augmented generation and a tool-using agent, we find that retrieval rather than generation accounts for most citation error, measured against each pair&amp;amp;rsquo;s source articles: given the gold article, the model cites it in 92% of cases, and retrieval alone reduces citations of non-existent norms from 31% to zero. The agent adds nothing on this measure and abstains far less often. Dense retrieval improves citation recall but nearly doubles the repealed norms presented as current; date-aware ranking removes them for every retriever, although under dense retrieval it also removes three historically required citations. The retrieval findings are reproduced on two open models.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-19</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 1042: A Dated Corpus and a Seven-Layer Benchmark for Kazakhstani Legal Question Answering</b></p>
	<p>Information <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1042">doi: 10.3390/info17101042</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Nurbol Beisov
		Nurlykhan Kalzhanov
		Arman Yeleussinov
		Maxatbek Satymbekov
		Zholdas Buribayev
		Yerbol Alimkulov
		Ualikhan Akhatov
		Yernur Oxikbayev
		</p>
	<p>Legal question answering is usually evaluated as if the law were static. In Kazakhstan, it changed twice within one year: a new Constitution replaced the 1995 text on 1 July 2026, and a new Tax Code replaced its predecessor on 1 January 2026, both reusing article numbers for different norms. We release a dated corpus of 41 Kazakhstani acts parsed to article level in both official languages (12,235 Russian and 11,973 Kazakh articles) and a benchmark of 150 question-answer pairs in seven layers, two of which score declining to answer and asking for a date. Two practising lawyers confirmed every pair. Comparing a no-retrieval baseline, retrieval-augmented generation and a tool-using agent, we find that retrieval rather than generation accounts for most citation error, measured against each pair&amp;amp;rsquo;s source articles: given the gold article, the model cites it in 92% of cases, and retrieval alone reduces citations of non-existent norms from 31% to zero. The agent adds nothing on this measure and abstains far less often. Dense retrieval improves citation recall but nearly doubles the repealed norms presented as current; date-aware ranking removes them for every retriever, although under dense retrieval it also removes three historically required citations. The retrieval findings are reproduced on two open models.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>A Dated Corpus and a Seven-Layer Benchmark for Kazakhstani Legal Question Answering</dc:title>
