        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1030">

	<title>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 1030: DyGRAV: Dynamic Grounding and Relational Reasoning for Vision&amp;ndash;Language Navigation</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1030</link>
	<description>Vision&amp;amp;ndash;Language Navigation (VLN) requires embodied agents to interpret natural-language instructions in 3D environments. While significant progress has been made, state-of-the-art agents often struggle with instructions that demand fine-grained visual understanding, correctly grounding specific object attributes, distinguishing between similar objects, and interpreting complex spatial relationships. Many methods rely on static visual features or on global cross-modal attention and lack mechanisms to dynamically resolve ambiguities during navigation. We propose DyGRAV (Dynamic Grounding and Relational Reasoning for VLN), which is a dynamic, uncertainty-aware framework that augments a strong VLN backbone with two adaptively triggered reasoning modules. The Contextual VLM Querying module detects grounding ambiguity for descriptive phrases (e.g., &amp;amp;ldquo;the ceramic bowl&amp;amp;rdquo;) and issues targeted queries to a vision&amp;amp;ndash;language model (e.g., CLIP) using current visual evidence, while the Incremental Relational Scene Graph (IRSG) constructs lightweight, task-relevant relational memory online to resolve complex spatial relations (e.g., &amp;amp;ldquo;the chair next to the window&amp;amp;rdquo;). A budget-aware gating mechanism regulates when these modules are invoked, optimizing the balance between reasoning quality and computational cost through value-per-cost selection and hard budget constraints. Comprehensive evaluations on R2R and RxR, along with a fine-grained diagnostic benchmark emphasizing attribute and relation understanding, show that DyGRAV achieves superior navigation accuracy on challenging instructions while maintaining bounded per-step compute budgets with 97.33% episode-level adherence.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-16</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 1030: DyGRAV: Dynamic Grounding and Relational Reasoning for Vision&amp;ndash;Language Navigation</b></p>
	<p>Information <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1030">doi: 10.3390/info17101030</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Zain Chaudhary
		Zohaib Chaudhary
		Hammad Ahmed
		Prabin Bajgai
		Rabab Abdelfattah
		Ahmed Sherif
		</p>
	<p>Vision&amp;amp;ndash;Language Navigation (VLN) requires embodied agents to interpret natural-language instructions in 3D environments. While significant progress has been made, state-of-the-art agents often struggle with instructions that demand fine-grained visual understanding, correctly grounding specific object attributes, distinguishing between similar objects, and interpreting complex spatial relationships. Many methods rely on static visual features or on global cross-modal attention and lack mechanisms to dynamically resolve ambiguities during navigation. We propose DyGRAV (Dynamic Grounding and Relational Reasoning for VLN), which is a dynamic, uncertainty-aware framework that augments a strong VLN backbone with two adaptively triggered reasoning modules. The Contextual VLM Querying module detects grounding ambiguity for descriptive phrases (e.g., &amp;amp;ldquo;the ceramic bowl&amp;amp;rdquo;) and issues targeted queries to a vision&amp;amp;ndash;language model (e.g., CLIP) using current visual evidence, while the Incremental Relational Scene Graph (IRSG) constructs lightweight, task-relevant relational memory online to resolve complex spatial relations (e.g., &amp;amp;ldquo;the chair next to the window&amp;amp;rdquo;). A budget-aware gating mechanism regulates when these modules are invoked, optimizing the balance between reasoning quality and computational cost through value-per-cost selection and hard budget constraints. Comprehensive evaluations on R2R and RxR, along with a fine-grained diagnostic benchmark emphasizing attribute and relation understanding, show that DyGRAV achieves superior navigation accuracy on challenging instructions while maintaining bounded per-step compute budgets with 97.33% episode-level adherence.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>DyGRAV: Dynamic Grounding and Relational Reasoning for Vision&amp;amp;ndash;Language Navigation</dc:title>
