        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1025">

	<title>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 1025: SparseRoute: Structured Sparse Variable Routing for High-Dimensional Multivariate Time Series Forecasting</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1025</link>
	<description>Forecasting high-dimensional multivariate time series requires models to represent dependencies across variables without making variable interactions prohibitively expensive. Full attention over variable-level tokens has a pairwise cost quadratic in the number of variables; channel-independent methods avoid this cost but omit explicit cross-variable interactions. SparseRoute addresses this design choice by assigning variables to a small set of learned subspaces and applying attention to their aggregated representations. A vector-quantization routing module produces hard variable-to-subspace assignments, while a straight-through estimator supplies surrogate gradients during training. A dual-path decoder combines subspace predictions broadcast to their assigned variables with variable-level predictions from a shared multilayer perceptron. We evaluate the model with three seeds on Traffic, Electricity, Solar-Energy, PEMS04, and PEMS07. SparseRoute obtains the lowest mean MAE for Traffic at horizon 96 and the lowest mean MSE at horizon 192; Crossformer leads on several other dataset&amp;amp;ndash;horizon pairs. Relative to a dense variable-token model with matched tokenization and backbone, routing changes the pairwise attention factor from the square of the variable count to the square of the subspace count; this theoretical comparison does not establish an end-to-end resource advantage over the external baselines.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-15</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 1025: SparseRoute: Structured Sparse Variable Routing for High-Dimensional Multivariate Time Series Forecasting</b></p>
	<p>Information <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1025">doi: 10.3390/info17101025</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Hongcheng Zhu
		Yu Fang
		</p>
	<p>Forecasting high-dimensional multivariate time series requires models to represent dependencies across variables without making variable interactions prohibitively expensive. Full attention over variable-level tokens has a pairwise cost quadratic in the number of variables; channel-independent methods avoid this cost but omit explicit cross-variable interactions. SparseRoute addresses this design choice by assigning variables to a small set of learned subspaces and applying attention to their aggregated representations. A vector-quantization routing module produces hard variable-to-subspace assignments, while a straight-through estimator supplies surrogate gradients during training. A dual-path decoder combines subspace predictions broadcast to their assigned variables with variable-level predictions from a shared multilayer perceptron. We evaluate the model with three seeds on Traffic, Electricity, Solar-Energy, PEMS04, and PEMS07. SparseRoute obtains the lowest mean MAE for Traffic at horizon 96 and the lowest mean MSE at horizon 192; Crossformer leads on several other dataset&amp;amp;ndash;horizon pairs. Relative to a dense variable-token model with matched tokenization and backbone, routing changes the pairwise attention factor from the square of the variable count to the square of the subspace count; this theoretical comparison does not establish an end-to-end resource advantage over the external baselines.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>SparseRoute: Structured Sparse Variable Routing for High-Dimensional Multivariate Time Series Forecasting</dc:title>
			<dc:creator>Hongcheng Zhu</dc:creator>
			<dc:creator>Yu Fang</dc:creator>
