        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1013">

	<title>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 1013: A Survey of Smart Contract Vulnerability Detection Based on Tree Representation</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1013</link>
	<description>Smart contracts, characterized by automation, transparency, and decentralization, can establish efficient, secure, and reliable blockchain fund management and data processing mechanisms, playing a crucial supporting role in blockchain application scenarios. However, security issues in smart contracts are increasingly prominent, making vulnerability detection a critical research focus; detection methods based on tree representation have emerged as a promising direction in this field. A comprehensive review of tree representation-based smart contract vulnerability detection methods is provided in this paper, aiming to offer new insights and theoretical support for smart contract security. First, a three-level vulnerability taxonomy covering 18 common smart contract vulnerability types is presented, categorized by the specific stage of the smart contract life cycle where the vulnerability originates: the Solidity code layer, the EVM execution layer, and the blockchain system layer. Second, four traditional detection paradigms&amp;amp;mdash;formal verification, symbolic execution, fuzz testing, and static analysis&amp;amp;mdash;are characterized by their core mechanisms and compared across multiple performance dimensions. Third, following the PRISMA systematic review guidelines, 23 primary studies on tree representation-based methods are selected and organized under a novel three-category taxonomy: pure AST sequentialization/traversal, tree-structured neural networks, and AST-to-graph transformations. The technical and application-level challenges of these methods are synthesized, and corresponding open research questions and improvement directions are discussed to inform future work.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-12</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 1013: A Survey of Smart Contract Vulnerability Detection Based on Tree Representation</b></p>
	<p>Information <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/1013">doi: 10.3390/info17101013</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Gongming Wang
		Junsong Zhou
		Bingsen Li
		Haiying Li
		</p>
	<p>Smart contracts, characterized by automation, transparency, and decentralization, can establish efficient, secure, and reliable blockchain fund management and data processing mechanisms, playing a crucial supporting role in blockchain application scenarios. However, security issues in smart contracts are increasingly prominent, making vulnerability detection a critical research focus; detection methods based on tree representation have emerged as a promising direction in this field. A comprehensive review of tree representation-based smart contract vulnerability detection methods is provided in this paper, aiming to offer new insights and theoretical support for smart contract security. First, a three-level vulnerability taxonomy covering 18 common smart contract vulnerability types is presented, categorized by the specific stage of the smart contract life cycle where the vulnerability originates: the Solidity code layer, the EVM execution layer, and the blockchain system layer. Second, four traditional detection paradigms&amp;amp;mdash;formal verification, symbolic execution, fuzz testing, and static analysis&amp;amp;mdash;are characterized by their core mechanisms and compared across multiple performance dimensions. Third, following the PRISMA systematic review guidelines, 23 primary studies on tree representation-based methods are selected and organized under a novel three-category taxonomy: pure AST sequentialization/traversal, tree-structured neural networks, and AST-to-graph transformations. The technical and application-level challenges of these methods are synthesized, and corresponding open research questions and improvement directions are discussed to inform future work.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>A Survey of Smart Contract Vulnerability Detection Based on Tree Representation</dc:title>
