        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/992">

	<title>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 992: Beyond Output Faithfulness: Claim-Centered Relational Auditability for Source-Grounded Generative AI</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/992</link>
	<description>Source-grounded generative AI can produce plausible outputs while leaving relations among source records, representations, retrieved evidence, claims, decisions, corrections, and downstream reuse difficult to reconstruct. This critical-integrative review synthesizes a purposively assembled corpus of 74 sources spanning archival and cultural-heritage applications, information retrieval and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), AI auditability, rights, governance, and provenance-oriented standards. Structured analytical extraction, evidential-role assessment, and cross-source relational synthesis organize recurrent problems around six lifecycle joins: Source-to-Representation, Representation-to-Retrieved-Evidence, Evidence-to-Claim, Claim-to-Decision, Decision-to-Correction, and Correction-to-Reuse. The article proposes a claim-centered relational audit profile that keeps retrieval and presentation, citation and substantive evidential support, actor identity and decision authority, and authoritative correction and downstream remediation analytically distinct. Typed relations and candidate structural audit measures support relation-specific inspection without equating structural auditability with evidential adequacy or factual correctness. Direct empirical support for Correction-to-Reuse is limited to recognition and handling of authoritative status changes; broader downstream propagation and remediation remain implementation-level extensions requiring validation. A constructed Library of Congress example illustrates claim segmentation, evidence binding, authority-sensitive correction, and bounded remediation. The contribution is a review-informed conceptual specification rather than a new provenance ontology, RAG benchmark, or validated auditing standard.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-08</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 992: Beyond Output Faithfulness: Claim-Centered Relational Auditability for Source-Grounded Generative AI</b></p>
	<p>Information <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/992">doi: 10.3390/info17100992</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Jinlan Wang
		Peisheng Liu
		</p>
	<p>Source-grounded generative AI can produce plausible outputs while leaving relations among source records, representations, retrieved evidence, claims, decisions, corrections, and downstream reuse difficult to reconstruct. This critical-integrative review synthesizes a purposively assembled corpus of 74 sources spanning archival and cultural-heritage applications, information retrieval and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), AI auditability, rights, governance, and provenance-oriented standards. Structured analytical extraction, evidential-role assessment, and cross-source relational synthesis organize recurrent problems around six lifecycle joins: Source-to-Representation, Representation-to-Retrieved-Evidence, Evidence-to-Claim, Claim-to-Decision, Decision-to-Correction, and Correction-to-Reuse. The article proposes a claim-centered relational audit profile that keeps retrieval and presentation, citation and substantive evidential support, actor identity and decision authority, and authoritative correction and downstream remediation analytically distinct. Typed relations and candidate structural audit measures support relation-specific inspection without equating structural auditability with evidential adequacy or factual correctness. Direct empirical support for Correction-to-Reuse is limited to recognition and handling of authoritative status changes; broader downstream propagation and remediation remain implementation-level extensions requiring validation. A constructed Library of Congress example illustrates claim segmentation, evidence binding, authority-sensitive correction, and bounded remediation. The contribution is a review-informed conceptual specification rather than a new provenance ontology, RAG benchmark, or validated auditing standard.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>Beyond Output Faithfulness: Claim-Centered Relational Auditability for Source-Grounded Generative AI</dc:title>
			<dc:creator>Jinlan Wang</dc:creator>
			<dc:creator>Peisheng Liu</dc:creator>
		<dc:identifier>doi: 10.3390/info17100992</dc:identifier>
