        <item rdf:about="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/985">

	<title>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 985: Two Timescales, Two Calibers: Auditing Consumer Adaptation in Algorithmically Mediated Environments</title>
	<link>https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/985</link>
	<description>Platform analytics often use between-consumer behavioral differences to infer longitudinal consumer adaptation, although such differences need not describe changes within the same consumers. This study examines whether a matched between&amp;amp;ndash;within contrast retains its substantive interpretation across defensible analytical constructions. In algorithmically mediated environments, the study builds on established within&amp;amp;ndash;between decomposition and embeds a matched-estimand Caliber Divergence Index (CDI) in a portability audit spanning joint uncertainty, behavioral-opportunity conditioning, temporal conditioning, and behavioral representation. The primary analysis uses a longitudinal panel of 60,423 consumers (242,771 consumer-wave observations). In the focal matched-estimand specification, the between-consumer and within-consumer associations are both negative but differ in magnitude (&amp;amp;beta;B=&amp;amp;minus;2.9957, &amp;amp;beta;W=&amp;amp;minus;3.5132; CDI&amp;amp;nbsp;=&amp;amp;nbsp;0.5174, BCa 95% CI&amp;amp;nbsp;[0.4757,&amp;amp;nbsp;0.5573]). Opportunity conditioning reverses the contrast (CDI&amp;amp;nbsp;=&amp;amp;minus;0.4812), as does relative wave-position adjustment (&amp;amp;minus;0.8077). Continuous elapsed-time adjustment reduces it to approximately zero (&amp;amp;minus;0.0007), whereas integer-month fixed effects retain a positive contrast (+0.5186). Replacing top-brand share with brand HHI attenuates the contrast (+0.0565), with a confidence interval that includes zero. Competing generative regimes produce distinct signatures under the examined parameterizations, but none reproduces the focal empirical CDI. Complementary analyses of historical reviews and consumer-level behavior delineate temporal, measurement, and construct boundaries. Together, the findings show that a precisely estimated between&amp;amp;ndash;within contrast does not necessarily support a portable substantive interpretation across defensible analytical constructions.</description>
	<pubDate>2026-10-06</pubDate>

	<content:encoded><![CDATA[
	<p><b>Information, Vol. 17, Pages 985: Two Timescales, Two Calibers: Auditing Consumer Adaptation in Algorithmically Mediated Environments</b></p>
	<p>Information <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2078-2489/17/10/985">doi: 10.3390/info17100985</a></p>
	<p>Authors:
		Dan Lu
		Hongwei Liu
		Xiuli Yu
		Dongdong Shi
		</p>
	<p>Platform analytics often use between-consumer behavioral differences to infer longitudinal consumer adaptation, although such differences need not describe changes within the same consumers. This study examines whether a matched between&amp;amp;ndash;within contrast retains its substantive interpretation across defensible analytical constructions. In algorithmically mediated environments, the study builds on established within&amp;amp;ndash;between decomposition and embeds a matched-estimand Caliber Divergence Index (CDI) in a portability audit spanning joint uncertainty, behavioral-opportunity conditioning, temporal conditioning, and behavioral representation. The primary analysis uses a longitudinal panel of 60,423 consumers (242,771 consumer-wave observations). In the focal matched-estimand specification, the between-consumer and within-consumer associations are both negative but differ in magnitude (&amp;amp;beta;B=&amp;amp;minus;2.9957, &amp;amp;beta;W=&amp;amp;minus;3.5132; CDI&amp;amp;nbsp;=&amp;amp;nbsp;0.5174, BCa 95% CI&amp;amp;nbsp;[0.4757,&amp;amp;nbsp;0.5573]). Opportunity conditioning reverses the contrast (CDI&amp;amp;nbsp;=&amp;amp;minus;0.4812), as does relative wave-position adjustment (&amp;amp;minus;0.8077). Continuous elapsed-time adjustment reduces it to approximately zero (&amp;amp;minus;0.0007), whereas integer-month fixed effects retain a positive contrast (+0.5186). Replacing top-brand share with brand HHI attenuates the contrast (+0.0565), with a confidence interval that includes zero. Competing generative regimes produce distinct signatures under the examined parameterizations, but none reproduces the focal empirical CDI. Complementary analyses of historical reviews and consumer-level behavior delineate temporal, measurement, and construct boundaries. Together, the findings show that a precisely estimated between&amp;amp;ndash;within contrast does not necessarily support a portable substantive interpretation across defensible analytical constructions.</p>
	]]></content:encoded>

	<dc:title>Two Timescales, Two Calibers: Auditing Consumer Adaptation in Algorithmically Mediated Environments</dc:title>
